+# Local Base Fork
+
+The old onboarding note is wrong about both the fork command and the health check.
+
+It says to run:
+
+```bash
+yarn fork base
+```
+
+In this checkout that does **not** pass `base` to Anvil. The root script only forwards to the
+Foundry workspace:
+
+```json
+// package.json
+"fork": "yarn foundry:fork",
+"foundry:fork": "yarn workspace @se-2/foundry fork",
+```
+
+The Foundry workspace script reads the second Yarn positional argument (`$1`), not the first
+one (`$0`):
+
+```json
+// packages/foundry/package.json
+"fork": "make fork FORK_URL=${1:-mainnet}",
+```
+
+So a dry run of the onboarding command resolves to a mainnet fork:
+
+```bash
+$ MAKEFLAGS=-n yarn fork base
+anvil --fork-url mainnet --chain-id 31337
+```
+
+That is why reads from Base USDC at `0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913` came back
+empty: the local node was alive, but it was forked from Ethereum mainnet, where that Base USDC
+contract is not deployed at that address.
+
+The repo does have a Base RPC alias:
+
+```toml
+# packages/foundry/foundry.toml
+[rpc_endpoints]
+mainnet = "https://eth-mainnet.alchemyapi.io/v2/${ALCHEMY_API_KEY}"
+base = "https://mainnet.base.org"
+```
+
+## Correct Command
+
+From the repo root, pass a throwaway first positional argument so `base` becomes `$1`:
+
+```bash
+yarn fork _ base
+```
+
+A dry run shows that this is the command that actually reaches the Base alias:
+
+```bash
+$ MAKEFLAGS=-n yarn fork _ base
+anvil --fork-url base --chain-id 31337
+```
+
+Equivalently, from the Foundry package you can bypass the Yarn positional-argument issue:
+
+```bash
+cd packages/foundry
+make fork FORK_URL=base
+```
+
+Both commands end up at the Makefile fork target:
+
+```make
+# packages/foundry/Makefile
+fork: setup-anvil-wallet
+	anvil --fork-url ${FORK_URL} --chain-id 31337
+```
+
+## Better Fork Check
+
+Do not use `cast chain-id` to decide whether the fork source is Base. This Makefile always starts
+Anvil with `--chain-id 31337`, so `cast chain-id` only proves that the local node is responding.
+It does not prove which upstream chain Anvil forked.
+
+Check for the Base state this project depends on instead:
+
+```bash
+cast code \
+  --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8545 \
+  0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913
+```
+
+That should return non-empty bytecode, not `0x`.
+
+Then check a real USDC read:
+
+```bash
+cast call \
+  --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8545 \
+  0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913 \
+  "decimals()(uint8)"
+```
+
+On a Base fork this returns:
+
+```text
+6
+```
+
+If the code is `0x` or the `decimals()` call returns empty/reverts, the local node may still be
+mining transactions, but it is not the Base fork needed for this checkout.

